{"name":"Dithered Amber","hex":"#feb308","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dithered","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Produced by dithering; consisting of bits in different colors placed side by side to simulate an intermediate color that is not in the palette, or the somewhat banded or mottled look that this can produce."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of dither"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dithered"},{"term":"amber","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Ambergris, the waxy product of the sperm whale. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}\n\nFormerly thought to be the product of a plant.","Formerly thought to be the product of a plant."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a brownish yellow colour, like that of most amber."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/amber"}],"entries":[{"title":"Dither","blocks":[{"text":"Dither or dithering is an intentionally applied form of noise used to randomize quantization error, preventing large-scale patterns such as color banding in images. Dither is routinely used in processing of both digital audio and video data, and is often one of the last stages of mastering audio to a CD."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dither","wikidata":"Q376493"},{"title":"Amber","blocks":[{"text":"Amber is fossilized tree resin. It has been appreciated for its color and natural beauty since Neolithic times, and worked as a gemstone since classical antiquity. Amber is used in jewelry and as a healing agent in folk medicine."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Amber2.jpg/330px-Amber2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amber","wikidata":"Q25381"},{"title":"Amber (color)","blocks":[{"text":"The color amber is a pure chroma color, located on the color wheel midway between the colors of yellow and orange. The color name is derived from the material also known as amber, which is commonly found in a range of yellow-orange-brown-red colors; likewise, as a color, amber can refer to a range of yellow-orange colors. In English, the first recorded use of the term as a color name, rather than a reference to the specific substance, was in 1500."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Ant_in_amber_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Ant_in_amber_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amber_(color)","wikidata":"Q679355"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Composite_image_demonstrating_dithering_effect.png/500px-Composite_image_demonstrating_dithering_effect.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Composite_image_demonstrating_dithering_effect.png/500px-Composite_image_demonstrating_dithering_effect.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Image on left is original. Center image is reduced to 16 colors. Right image also 16 colors, but dithered to reduce banding effect.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AComposite_image_demonstrating_dithering_effect.png"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/aa/Reducing_amplitude_resolution_plot.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/aa/Reducing_amplitude_resolution_plot.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AReducing_amplitude_resolution_plot.png"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e3/Dithering_example_undithered.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https